/*
  Layer 2 -- the demand lexicon: maps an action (the !Step tag) to a usability dimension + level, the
  HCI judgment stated ONCE (with a citation) instead of baked into each detector. The lookup detectors
  read a row + !AtLeast(lvl, thr) (the band; order in core/types.spthy) so a level below threshold
  provably does not fire. Rows below (all 'hi'):
    compute  Temporal + Mental hi  -- key derivation / passphrase is slow + hard (Sweller 1988; TLX)
    compare  Effort hi             -- crypto has no physical analog (Whitten & Tygar 1999)
    authorize Arousal hi           -- high-stakes op; over-arousal freezes (Yerkes-Dodson 1908)
    share    Temporal + Mental hi  -- the email password disclosure step
  A profile SWAPS this for an interface / population demand profile to shift a level (P7/P9). Seeded
  once (persistent, read-only -> closed, no sources loop; OnceLexicon bounds it).
*/

rule Seed_lexicon_tlx [color=#9b51e0]:
    [ ]
  --[ SeedLexicon() ]->
    [ !Demands('compute',   'TemporalDemand', 'hi'),
      !Demands('compute',   'MentalDemand',   'hi'),
      !Demands('compare',   'Effort',         'hi'),
      !Demands('authorize', 'Arousal',        'hi'),
      // a 'share' step (disclosing a secret over a side channel) is both mentally demanding and under
      // deadline, so sigma1/sigma3 fire on it -- the secure_email password step is a 'share' action.
      !Demands('share',     'MentalDemand',   'hi'),
      !Demands('share',     'TemporalDemand', 'hi') ]

restriction OnceLexicon:
  "All #i #j. SeedLexicon()@i & SeedLexicon()@j ==> #i = #j"
